    {
      "content": "When a Bash-tool call pairs a shell-level timeout N with a long-running command, ALWAYS also raise the Bash tool's own timeout parameter well above N (e.g. 600000 for N=540). Leaving the tool-level timeout at its 120000ms default while the shell command runs past that mark causes the harness to auto-background the call, orphaning the process tree under systemd --user rather than cleanly returning or terminating it -- the process keeps running, can hold locks (e.g. a repo-level gate-lock), and contends with later foreground attempts.",
